{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"maximum likelihood estimation maximum likelihood estimation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","fileName":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 21:47\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Bayesian Parameter and Structure Learning]] · [[Count Models and Empirical Bayes]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Bayesian Parameter and Structure Learning","Count Models and Empirical Bayes","bayesianprogramming.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"maximum likelihood estimation maximum likelihood estimation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","fileName":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation","heading":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Maximum likelihood estimation selects the parameter value under which the observed data has greatest probability. It converts parameter identification into an optimization problem once a parametric family has been specified.\n\nThe result is a point estimate and does not by itself retain uncertainty about nearby values. Unlike a Bayesian estimator, it does not combine the likelihood with an explicit prior over parameters.\n\nThe source illustrates the method with Poisson counts: independence turns the joint probability of all observed segment counts into a likelihood for the rate parameter, and the maximizing rate is the sample mean count. The example shows how a distributional assumption determines the optimization target.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Bayesian Parameter and Structure Learning","Count Models and Empirical Bayes","bayesianprogramming.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"maximum likelihood estimation maximum likelihood estimation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","fileName":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[bayesianprogramming.pdf]]\n\n[[dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Bayesian Parameter and Structure Learning","Count Models and Empirical Bayes","bayesianprogramming.pdf","dataanalysisforthelifescienceswithr.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}